# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Research output stays on this machine.
#
# The repo is public. These directories contain the seed topics, the niche
# rankings and DECISION.md — i.e. which app is about to get built. That is not
# a security risk (no credentials are involved anywhere in this pipeline) but it
# is a competitive one. Schema is documented via the *.example.csv files, which
# are committed and use a throwaway seed topic.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
data/*
!data/*.example.csv
!data/.gitkeep

output/*
!output/.gitkeep

manual/NOTES*.md

# caches and databases
*.db
*.db-journal
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# python
venv/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# secrets — nothing here needs one today, but never let that change silently
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
credentials.json

# os / editor
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/

# Your actual config holds your seed topics — i.e. which niches you are
# researching. Copy config.example.yaml to config.yaml and edit that.
config.yaml

# Archived previous runs — same competitive-secrecy reasoning as data/ and output/.
runs/

# Internal working notes. GitHub cannot make a single file private inside a
# public repo, so private/ lives in the working copy and is never pushed. Same
# mechanism as data/ and runs/ above. Check with: git check-ignore -v private/
private/
IMPROVEMENTS*.md
NOTES*.md
notes/
